          %h2#Idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em Dr. von Neumann, ich möchte gerne wissen, was ist denn eigentlich ein Hilbertscher Raum ?
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            A Hilbert space is (see Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HilbertSpace"}
            for details):
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real
                or, usually,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            %li
              %p
                equipped with a positive definite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            %li
              %p
                which, as a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+space"} complete
                with respect to the induced
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
          %p
            Hilbert spaces are central to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            and specifically to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} spaces of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+quantum+states"} pure quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
            for one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
            to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collapse+of+the+wavefunction"}> collapse
            ” to another one under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimension
            (as is the case of interest in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiniteDimensionalInnerProductSpaces"}
            below), otherwise it naturally encodes the possibility of an infinite number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} measurement
            outcomes.
          %p
            Hilbert spaces with (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+map"}> bounded
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            between them form a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"}> dagger
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"}> category
            , often denoted
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilb"} Hilb
            or similar, with the
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-category"} dagger
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+maps"} bounded linear maps
            to their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+operators"} adjoint operators
            with respect to the Hermitian inner product. Finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} dagger-compact category
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/an+elementary+treatment+of+Hilbert+spaces"}> an elementary treatment of Hilbert spaces
              \.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p We first state the
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#StandardDefinition"} standard formulation
          %p of the definition of Hilbert spaces and then various equivalent
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AlternativeDefinitions"} alternative formulations
          %h3#StandardDefinition Standard formulation
          %p We first state the definition as such and then recall the ingredients that go into it:
          %p
          #HilbertSpace.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (Hilbert space)
              %br/
            %p
              A
              %em Hilbert space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big(\mathscr{H}, \langle -,-\rangle\big)
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex
                  or
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                  (possibly infinite-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"}> dimensional
                  ),
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,- \rangle
                  (Def.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
            %p which is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  complete (Def.
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompleteInnerProduct"}
              %li
                %p
                  positive definite (Def.
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefiniteInnerProduct"}
            %p
          %p
          #MorphismOfHilbertSpaces.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (morphisms of Hilbert spaces)
              %br/
              There are different notions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              between
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %p
              At the very least, a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
              between the underlying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+maps"} bounded linear maps
              or sometimes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+maps"} short maps
              (see at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "Banach+space#morphisms"} maps of Banach spaces
            %p
              Instead of being fully respected by maps, the inner product on Hilbert spaces induces the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-category"} dagger
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
              on their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-spaces"} hom-spaces
              given by sending any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+map"} bounded linear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \colon \mathscr{H}_1 \to \mathscr{H}_2
              to its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+operator"} adjoint operator
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo †
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^\dagger \colon \mathscr{H}_2 \to \mathscr{H}_1
            #eq:AdjointnessRelation.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo †
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle A^\dagger(-), -\rangle_{1}
                    \\;=\;
                    \\langle -, A(-)\rangle_{2}
            %p
          %p
          #FiniteDimensionalInnerProductSpaces.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"}> finite-dimensional Hilbert space
                )
              %br/
              In the case that the underlying vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              happens to be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional vector space
            %p
              A
              %em finite-dimensional Hilbert space
              is equivalently a positive-definite
              = succeed "-space" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermitian+inner+product"} hermitian inner product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            %p
              Finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} dagger-compact category
              and play a central role in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            We recall now the meaning of the concepts entering Def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HilbertSpace"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in \mathbb{C}
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{z}
            for its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugate"} complex conjugate
          %p
          #AlternativeGroundFields.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (alternative ground fields)
              %br/
              The following applies verbatim also for the ground field of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %a{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"} below
              becomes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+form"} bilinear
              and one speaks of
              = succeed "." do
                %em real Hilbert spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              are the only possible
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+fields"} ground fields
              for Hilbert spaces (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://math.stackexchange.com/a/4184099/58526"} MO:a/4184099
          %p
          #HermitianInnerProduct.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (Hermitian inner product)
              %br/
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              is a function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle {-},{-} \rangle
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathscr{H} \times \mathscr{H} \to \mathbb{C}
            %p that is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %strong> sesquilinear
                  \:
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo =
                            %mn 0
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 0, x \rangle = 0
                      and
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 0
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, 0 \rangle = 0
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi x
                            %mo +
                            %mi y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x + y, z \rangle = \langle x, z \rangle + \langle y, z \rangle
                      and
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi y
                              %mo +
                              %mi z
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo +
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi z
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y + z \rangle = \langle x, y \rangle + \langle x, z \rangle
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi c
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo =
                            %mover
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle c x, y \rangle = \bar{c} \langle x, y \rangle
                      and
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo =
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, c y \rangle = c \langle x, y \rangle
              %li
                %p
                  %strong> conjugate-symmetric
                  \:
                #eq:ConjugateSymmetryOfInnerProduct.maruku-equation
                  %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo =
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\langle x, y \rangle = \overline{\langle y, x \rangle}
            %p
          %p
          #ConventionsForInnerProduct.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (convention for the inner product)
              %br/
              Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
              uses the
              %em physicist's convention
              that the inner product is conjugate-linear in the first variable rather than in the second, instead of the
              = succeed "," do
                %em mathematician's convention
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-Hilbert+space"} Hilbert spaces
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The axiom list in Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
              is rather redundant. First of all, (1.1) follows from (1.3) by setting
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              and that the inner product is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
              (which, as we will see, is always true anyway for a Hilbert space).
          %p
          #DefiniteInnerProduct.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (definite inner product)
              %br/
              Given an inner product according to Def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
            #eq:NormSquare.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                                  %mn 2
                                %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                                %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mo ↪
                            %mi ℂ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi v
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi v
                            %mo ,
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathllap{
                    \\|{-}\|^2
                    \\;\colon\;
                    }
                    \\mathscr{H} &amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\mathbb{R}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    \\mathbb{C}
                    \\\
                    v &amp;\mapsto&amp;
                    \\langle v, v \rangle
                    }
            %p
              Notice that this takes only real values, by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConjugateSymmetryOfInnerProduct"} (2)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
              is called:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong> positive semidefinite
                  , or simply
                  = succeed "," do
                    %strong positive
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi v
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|v\|^2 \geq 0
                  for all
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi v
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \mathscr{H}
              %li
                %p
                  %strong non-degenerate
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi v
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|v\|^2 = 0
                  implies that
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi v
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v = 0
              %li
                %p
                  %strong positive definite
                  if it is both positive and non-degenerate.
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              An inner product is called
              %em indefinite
              if some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi v
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|v\|^2
              are positive and some are negative.
          %p
          #CompleteInnerProduct.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (complete inner product)
              %br/
              The inner product (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
              %strong complete
              if, given any infinite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v_1, v_2, \ldots)
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{V}
              such that we have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            #eq:Cauchy.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo ‖
                        %munderover
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo =
                            %mi m
                          %mrow
                            %mi m
                            %mo +
                            %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi i
                        %mo ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lim_{m,n\to\infty}
                    \\left\|\sum_{i=m}^{m+n} v_i\right\|^2 = 0
                    \\,,
            %p
              then there exists a (necessarily unique)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              such that
            #eq:converge.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo ‖
                        %mi S
                        %mo −
                        %munderover
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                          %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi i
                        %mo ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lim_{n\to\infty} \left\|S
                    \-
                    \\sum_{i=1}^n v_i\right\|^2 = 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              If the inner product is definite (Def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefiniteInnerProduct"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S = \sum_{i=1}^\infty v_i
            %p (with the right-hand side undefined if no such sum exists).
            %p
          %h3#AlternativeDefinitions Alternative formulations
          %h4#AsBanachSpacesAndMetricSpaces As Banach spaces and metric spaces
          %p
            If an inner product (Def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HermitianInnerProduct"}
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefiniteInnerProduct"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            of the norm square
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi v
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|v\|^2 = \langle v, v \rangle
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NormSquare"} (3)
            being the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|v\|
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \mathscr{H}
          %p
            This norm satisfies all of the requirements of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            %em parallelogram law
          #eq:ParallelogramLaw.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\|v_1 + v_2\|^2
                  +
                  \\|v_1 - v_2\|^2
                  \\;=\;
                  2 \|v_1\|^2 + 2 \|v_2\|^2
                  \\,.
          %p which not all Banach spaces need satisfy. (The name of this law comes from its geometric interpretation: the norms in the left-hand side are the lengths of the diagonals of a parallelogram, while the norms in the right-hand side are the lengths of the sides.)
          %p
            Furthermore, any Banach space satsifying the parallelogram law
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ParallelogramLaw"} (6)
            has a unique inner product that reproduces the norm, defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 4
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle v_1, v_2 \rangle
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\frac{1}{4}
                  \\left(
                  \\|v_1 + v_2\|^2
                  \-
                  \\|v_1 - v_2\|^2
                  \-
                  \\mathrm{i}
                  \\|v_1 + \mathrm{i}v_2\|^2
                  +
                  \\mathrm{i}
                  \\|v_1 - \mathrm{i}v_2\|^2
                  \\right) ,
          %p
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2}(\|v_1 + v_2\|^2 - \|v_1 - v_2\|^2)
            in the real case.
          %p
            Therefore: A Hilbert space is equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            that satisfies the parallelogram law
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ParallelogramLaw"} (6)
          %p This actually works a bit more generally; a positive semidefinite inner product space is a pseudonormed vector space that satisfies the parallelogram law. (We cannot, however, recover an indefinite inner product from a norm.)
          %p
            Moreover, in any positive semidefinite inner product space, let the
            %strong distance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y)
            be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d(x,y)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\|y - x\|
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudometric"} pseudometric
          %p
            In fact, the axioms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            (or pseudonormed vector space) can be written entirely in terms of the metric; we can also state the parallelogram law as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y)^2 + d(x,-y)^2 = 2 d(x,0)^2 + 2 d(x,x+y)^2 .
          %p
            In definitions, it is probably most common to see the metric introduced only to state the completeness requirement. Indeed,
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Cauchy"} (4)
            says that the sequence of partial sums is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:converge"} (5)
            says that the sequence of partial sums converges to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#banach_spaces Banach spaces
          %p
            All of the
            = succeed "-parametrised" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            apply if you take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 2
          %p
            In particular, the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            is a complex Hilbert space with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi u
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi u
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle = \sum_{u=1}^n \bar{x}_u y_u .
          %p
            Any subfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            gives a positive definite inner product space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^n
            whose completion is either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+space"} cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is a real Hilbert space; the geometric notions of distance and angle defined above agree with ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean geometry
            for this example.
          %h3#of_lebesgue_squareintegrable_functions_over_a_manifold Of Lebesgue square-integrable functions over a manifold
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-2-spaces"} L-
            Hilbert spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(\mathbb{R})
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2([0,1])
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(\mathbb{R}^3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(X)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            consists of the almost-everywhere defined functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the scalar field (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int |f|^2
            converges to a finite number, with functions identified if they are equal almost everywhere; we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∫
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, g\rangle = \int \bar{f} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
          %h3#of_squareintegrable_halfdensities Of square-integrable half-densities
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+Hilbert+space+of+half-densities"} canonical Hilbert space of half-densities
          %h2#Properties Properties
          %h3#bases Bases
          %p
            A basic result is that abstractly, Hilbert spaces of the same dimension are all of the same type: every Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            admits an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthonormal+basis"} orthonormal basis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq H
            whose inclusion map extends (necessarily uniquely) to an isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi l
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2(S) \to H
          %p
            of Hilbert spaces. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi l
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2(S)
            is the vector space consisting of those
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to the scalar field such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi x
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi u
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|x\|^2 = \sum_{u: S} |x_u|^2
          %p
            converges to a finite number; this may also be obtained by completing the vector space of formal linear combinations of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with an inner product uniquely determined by the rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mi v
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle u, v \rangle = \delta_{u v} \qquad u, v \in S
          %p
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_{u v}
            denotes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kronecker+delta"} Kronecker delta
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2(S)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi u
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi u
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle = \sum_{u: S} \bar{x}_u y_u .
          %p (This sum converges by the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality.)
          %p
            In general, this result uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (usually in the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable
            Hilbert spaces needs only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
            and so is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            by most schools' standards. Even without dependent choice, explicit orthornormal bases for particular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(X)
            can often be produced using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/approximation+of+the+identity"} approximation of the identity
            techniques, often in concert with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gram-Schmidt+process"} Gram-Schmidt process
          %p
            In particular, all infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert spaces are abstractly isomorphic to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2(\mathbb{N})
          %h3#cauchyschwarz_inequality Cauchy–Schwarz inequality
          %p
            The
            %em Schwarz inequality
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em Cauchy–Буняковский–Schwarz inequality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\langle x, y \rangle| \leq \|x\| \|y\| .
          %p
            This is really two theorems (at least): an abstract theorem that the inequality holds in any Hilbert space, and concrete theorems that it holds when the inner product and norm are defined by the formulas used in the examples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(X)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi l
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2(S)
            above. The concrete theorems apply even to functions that don't belong to the Hilbert space and so prove that the inner product converges whenever the norms converge. (A somewhat stronger result is needed to conclude this convergence constructively; it may be found in Errett Bishop's book.)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite-dimensional Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigged+Hilbert+space"} rigged Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+C-star-module"}> Hilbert C-star-module
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+bimodule"} Hilbert bimodule
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+field+of+Hilbert+spaces"} measurable field of Hilbert spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+vector+space"} Kähler vector space
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Hilbert spaces were effectively introduced and used by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} David Hilbert
            and others in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            theory, but the terminology and the formal definition is due to:
          %ul
            %li#vonNeumann30
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
              , §I in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Allgemeine Eigenwerttheorie Hermitescher Funktionaloperatoren
              %strong 102
              (1930) 49–131 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01782338"}> doi:10.1007/BF01782338
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            motivated from laying foundations for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li#vonNeumann32
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                \:
              %p
                %em> Mathematische Grundlagen der Quantenmechanik
                , Springer (1932, 1971) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %p
                %em Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Mechanics
                Princeton University Press (1955) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400889921"}> doi:10.1515/9781400889921
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_Foundations_of_Quantum_Mechanics"} Wikipedia entry
          %p (where the definition occupies section II.1)
          %p but see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miklos+R%C3%A9dei"}> Miklos Rédei
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Why John von Neumann did not Like the Hilbert Space formalism of quantum mechanics (and what he liked instead)
              %strong 27
              4 (1996) 493-510 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S1355-2198(96)00017-2"}> doi:10.1016/S1355-2198(96)00017-2
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Early history:
          %ul
            %li#MacLane88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hilbert space
              = succeed "," do
                %em Concepts and Categories in Perspective
              %em A century of mathematics in America
              Part 1, AMS (1988) 323-365. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/samplings/math-history/hmath1-maclane25.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/publicoutreach/math-history/hmath1-index"} ISBN:0-8218-0124-4
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolas+Bourbaki"}> Nicolas Bourbaki
              , §V in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological Vector Spaces
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61715-7"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-61715-7
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Mackey"}> George Mackey
                ,
                %em The Mathematical Foundations of Quamtum Mechanics
                A Lecture-note Volume, The mathematical physics monograph series. Princeton university (1963)
            %li
              %p
                E. Prugoveĉki,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Quantum mechanics in Hilbert Space
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatic
            characterization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-category"} dagger-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilb"} Hilb
            of Hilbert spaces, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            between them:
          %ul
            %li#HeunenKornell21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Heunen"}> Chris Heunen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Kornell"} Andre Kornell
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axioms for the category of Hilbert spaces
                %strong 119
                9 (2022) e2117024119 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07418"}> arXiv:2109.07418
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2117024119"} doi:10.1073/pnas.2117024119
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Heunen"}> Chris Heunen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Kornell"} Andre Kornell
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nesta+van+der+Schaaf"} Nesta van der Schaaf
                %em Axioms for the category of Hilbert spaces and linear contractions
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02688"}> arXiv:2211.02688
                \&amp;rbrack;
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/analysis"} analysis
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  %em Dr. von Neumann, I would like to know what is a Hilbert space?
                  – Question asked by
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} David Hilbert
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
                  in Göttingen (cf.
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "#vonNeumann30"} von Neumann 1930, §I
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#MacLane88"} MacLane 1988, §5
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
